  "id": "COMPOUND:23663 - PROTEIN:4953",
  "verbalization": "Phellandral, (R)- is implicated in the pathogenesis of HEp-2 cells through multiple distinct mechanisms, all of which involve the modulation of cellular processes critical to the survival and proliferation of the cell line. The first pathway links Phellandral to (S)-2-haloacid dehalogenase, which is targeted by N-(3,5-dibromo-4-hydroxyphenyl)-4-hydroxy-3,5-dimethylbenzamide, ultimately leading to HEp-2 cell death. The second pathway connects Phellandral to UDP-glucuronosyltransferase 2B15, which is targeted by Aeruginosin DA722, also resulting in HEp-2 cell inhibition. The third pathway shows Phellandral targeting CDK3/Cyclin E, which is inhibited by 2-(3,4-Dihydroxyphenyl)Acetic Acid, again affecting HEp-2 viability. These findings suggest that Phellandral exerts its effects through diverse molecular pathways that disrupt key cellular functions in HEp-2 cells. The integration of these mechanisms highlights the potential of Phellandral as a multi-targeted agent with broad biological impact on HEp-2 cells, though further validation of these pathways in clinical settings is required to establish its therapeutic potential.",
